 "lines": [
  {
   "text": "Rolf's kite looped so many times, it tied him to his goat.",
   "s": 0.12,
   "e": 3.019
  },
  {
   "text": "Rolf bet Bea his kite didn't need a tail. \"Tails are for dogs!\" So why do kites have tails?",
   "s": 3.219,
   "e": 7.997
  },
  {
   "text": "Loser buys lunch: one cheese sandwich and two pickles.",
   "s": 8.297,
   "e": 11.133
  },
  {
   "text": "Bea's kite had a tail of nine ribbons. Pretzel the goat wanted to eat all nine.",
   "s": 11.333,
   "e": 15.404
  },
  {
   "text": "Rolf's kite went up, spun like a washing machine, and dove into the duck pond.",
   "s": 15.604,
   "e": 19.483
  },
  {
   "text": "So Rolf gave it a bigger sail. \"Tails are for dogs!\"",
   "s": 19.683,
   "e": 22.478
  },
  {
   "text": "It spun so hard, it lifted Rolf off his boots and wrapped him to Pretzel.",
   "s": 22.598,
   "e": 25.966
  },
  {
   "text": "Meanwhile, Bea's kite sat up there for forty minutes, calm as a cloud.",
   "s": 26.166,
   "e": 29.657
  },
  {
   "text": "So what does the tail do?",
   "s": 29.857,
   "e": 30.895
  },
  {
   "text": "Wind drags the tail backward, so the kite's nose always points into the wind.",
   "s": 31.195,
   "e": 35.213
  },
  {
   "text": "It works like the feathers on an arrow. No tail, and every gust spins it.",
   "s": 35.613,
   "e": 39.331
  },
  {
   "text": "Bea won the pickles. Then Pretzel ate her tail.",
   "s": 39.531,
   "e": 41.602
  },
  {
   "text": "Now she's tied to the goat too.",
   "s": 41.802,
   "e": 43.302
  }
 ],
